  <bioghist id="db1fbe06dfad68b8d083823995e3f1a7">
    <head>Biographical / Historical</head>
<p>Karen McGuinness is a university administrator who worked for several years at the Ford Foundation. McGuiness joined the Foundation as an Assistant Program Officer in the Developing Country Programs in 1986. While in that position, she focused on grantmaking for projects and initiatives concerned with human rights, child survival, and women in Asia. She then moved to the Developing Country Programs' specific India, Nepal, and Sri Lanka Program. Following a change in the Foundation's program structure, the India, Nepal, and Sri Lanka Program was put under Asia Programs, and McGuiness was promoted to Program Officer.</p><p>McGuinness' career at Princeton University started shortly after she left the Ford Foundation. From 1994 to 2004, she lectured at the university on subjects such as public policy, development, and gender. In 2004, she was appointed Associate Dean for Graduate Education for the School of Public and International Affairs. She also served as Assistant Dean for Graduate Education for the larger university between 2004 and 2011.</p><p>McGuinness earned a B.A. in economics from Trinity Washington University, an M.P.A. in development studies from Princeton University, and a M.A. in government from Cornell University.</p>  </bioghist>
